Abstraction in Java

1).Delivering only essential information to the outside world while hiding background details.
2).It is a design and programming concept that separates interface from implementation.
3).Users can use functionalities without knowing the actual internal working or implementation.
4).Real-life example:

->Using AirPods without knowing how they internally work.
->Driving a car without understanding the engine mechanism.
5).Main purpose:
Reduce complexity
Increase security by hiding unnecessary details
Improve code maintainability and reusability


Abstraction in Collections
1).sort() method is used to sort arrays, lists, or collections of items.
2).We only use the method to sort data, but we do not know the internal sorting algorithm used.
3).The implementation details are hidden from the user, which is an example of abstraction.
4).Example:
->Collections.sort(numbers);
It sorts the list automatically without exposing internal working.
5).Benefits:
->Simplifies programming
->Hides complex implementation
->Makes code easier to use and maintain


What is Abstract Class?
1).An abstract class in Java is a class that cannot be instantiated (object cannot be created directly).
2).It is designed to be inherited or subclassed by other classes.
3).An abstract class acts as a superclass for classes that share common properties or behavior.
4).It provides abstraction by defining a common structure or interface for subclasses.
5).It may contain:
->Abstract methods (without implementation)
->Concrete methods (with implementation)
6).Complete implementation details are provided by the child classes.
7).Abstract class concept is based on the idea of Abstraction in Java.



