Design Strategy
1).Abstraction divides code into two parts:
Interface
Implementation
2).The interface should remain separate from the implementation.
3).If the internal implementation changes, the interface can still remain the same.
4).Programs using the interface remain unaffected by implementation changes.
5).Only recompilation with the latest implementation may be required.
6).Abstraction helps in:
-Modular programming
-Better maintainability
-Flexible and reusable code design


## Abstract Class vs Interface

| Feature                  | Abstract Class                                                 | Interface                                                                                           |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Instantiation**        | Cannot be instantiated directly                                | Cannot be instantiated                                                                              |
| **Method Types**         | Can have both abstract and concrete methods                    | Before Java 8: only abstract methods. From Java 8 onwards: can also have default and static methods |
| **Constructor**          | Can have constructors                                          | Cannot have constructors                                                                            |
| **Access Modifiers**     | Methods can use public, protected, or private access modifiers | Methods are implicitly public. From Java 9, private methods are also allowed inside interfaces      |
| **Inheritance**          | A class can extend only one abstract class                     | A class can implement multiple interfaces                                                           |
| **Fields and Constants** | Can have fields and constants with any access modifier         | All fields are implicitly public, static, and final                                                 |
| **Use Case**             | Used when classes share common properties and behavior         | Used to define a contract for multiple classes                                                      |

## Advantages of Abstraction

1. Reduces Complexity

2. Increases Security

3. Increases Reusability

4. Avoids Code Duplication

5. Supports Loose Coupling





